.TH ATD 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
atd \- run jobs queued by at and batch
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B atd
.RB [ \-l
.IR load ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR interval ]
.RB [ \-f | \-d ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B atd
runs the jobs that
.BR at (1)
and
.BR batch (1)
place in the spool directory
.IR /var/spool/at .
.PP
Each queue is a subdirectory named by one letter: the lowercase queues
.BR a " to " z
and the uppercase queues
.BR A " to " Z .
Queue
.B b
and the uppercase queues are batch queues; the others are
.B at
queues.
A job is a regular file in a queue directory whose name consists of letters,
digits and dots; its modification time is the time it is due.
.PP
On each pass
.B atd
scans the queues in the order
.BR a \(en z ,
then
.BR A \(en Z .
In an
.B at
queue every job that is due is run.
In a batch queue at most one due job is run per pass, and only when the
first number in
.I /proc/loadavg
is no greater than the load limit and at least
.I interval
seconds have passed since the last batch job ran.
If
.I /proc/loadavg
cannot be read, no batch jobs run.
If any job ran, another pass starts at once; otherwise
.B atd
sleeps
.I interval
seconds (1 second if
.I interval
is 0).
.PP
To run a job,
.B atd
moves it into
.I /var/spool/at/=
and forks a child, which starts a new session, sets its group and user ID
to the owner of the job file, sends standard output and standard error to
.IR /var/spool/at/spool/ job .out ,
and executes
.B /bin/sh
.IR job-file .
.B atd
waits for the job to finish, then removes the job file.
Jobs therefore run one at a time.
.PP
By default
.B atd
forks, the parent exits 0, and the child calls
.BR setsid (2)
and runs in the background.
It writes no pid file.
At boot,
.I /etc/rc.d/30\-atd
starts it as
.B "atd \-f"
in the background, with output to
.IR /dev/null ,
and records the pid in
.IR /var/run/atd.pid .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-l " load"
Run batch jobs only when the load average is at most
.IR load ,
which must be greater than 0.
The default is 1.5.
.TP
.BI \-b " interval"
Minimum number of seconds between batch jobs, and the time to sleep between
passes.
The default is 60.
A negative value is an error.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-d
Stay in the foreground.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B atd
does not exit while it is running normally.
.TP
.B 0
The parent process after a successful fork.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or option value, or the fork or
.BR setsid (2)
failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /var/spool/at/ q /
Queue
.IR q .
.TP
.I /var/spool/at/=
Jobs currently running.
.TP
.I /var/spool/at/spool/
Captured job output,
.IR job .out.
.TP
.I /proc/loadavg
Load average for the batch queues.
.TP
.I /etc/rc.d/30\-atd
Boot script.
.SH EXAMPLE
Run batch jobs whenever the load is below 0.8, checking every 30 seconds,
in the foreground:
.EX
# atd \-f \-l 0.8 \-b 30
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR at (1),
.BR batch (1),
.BR init (8)
.SH BUGS
An
.B at
job can start up to
.I interval
seconds (60 by default) after it is due, because that is how long
.B atd
sleeps between passes.
.PP
Output is never mailed; the
.I .out
files are left in
.I /var/spool/at/spool
and never removed.
.PP
The job's environment, working directory and umask as recorded by
.BR at (1)
are not restored; jobs run with umask 0, in
.BR atd 's
working directory and environment.
.PP
The return values of
.BR setgid (2)
and
.BR setuid (2)
are not checked, and supplementary groups are not changed.
The output file is created after the user ID is changed, so if the job's
owner cannot write to
.IR /var/spool/at/spool ,
the output goes wherever
.BR atd 's
does.
